      "text": "$\\log_x 4=\\log_{27} 3$. Let $x=\\boxed{\\text{answer}}$.\n\nThe solution is reasonable based upon the problem.\nTo solve the given logarithmic equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we will use the change of base formula for logarithms. The change of base formula states that \\(\\log_b a = \\frac{\\log_c a}{\\log_c b}\\) for any positive \\(c\\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite \\(\\log_x 4\\) and \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) using the change of base formula with base 10 (common logarithm):\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this equation, we need to express the logarithms in terms of base 2. Note that:\n\n\\[\n\\log 4 = \\log (2^2) = 2 \\log 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\log 27 = \\log (3^3) = 3 \\log 3\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into our equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\log 2}{\\log x} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\log 2}{\\log x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(\\log x\\), cross-multiply by \\(\\log x\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\log 2 \\cdot 3 = \\log x\n\\]\n\n\\[\n6 \\log 2 = \\log x\n\\]\n\nNow, divide both sides by \\(\\log 2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\log x = 6 \\cdot \\log 2\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log 2\\) is a constant, let's denote it as \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\log x = 6k\n\\]\n\nExponentiate both sides to solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = 10^{6k}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10^{6 \\log_2 2}}\n\\]",
